Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:01.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.40,0:00:03.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Welcome to the Bipartisan Policy Center.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.16,0:00:09.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm Leslie John Taras Semay and together with my colleague Anne Fishman, we lead the energy program here at BPC.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.43,0:00:13.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we're so glad that you're here to cap off a really big week this week.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.20,0:00:15.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was national clean it energy week.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.79,0:00:22.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was New York climate week and really the future of energy in America has never been so much in the spotlight.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.54,0:00:26.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're really excited to continue that conversation here today with all of you.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.84,0:00:30.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,The BPC energy program has been a driving force in federal policy for over 16 years and
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.98,0:00:37.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,we're really leading the way on building a strong bipartisan foundation of support for technology and innovation,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.62,0:00:43.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,for decarbonization, permitting reform, critical minerals, carbon management, so much more.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.24,0:00:49.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,And these are just pieces of a larger puzzle of how to ensure that all Americans have access to energy that is secure,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.68,0:00:52.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,reliable, affordable, and clean.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.32,0:01:00.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so we're here today that because the growth of AI is a new puzzle piece that we didn't even know that we needed to account for until recently.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.53,0:01:01.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, you know, where where do we put it?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.03,0:01:03.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where does AI go in all of this?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.87,0:01:09.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so we're going to dig into these questions and more as part of a new BPC project that we're launching today on
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.99,0:01:21.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,AI and energy and I'm so excited to invite my colleague Tanya Das to the stage here to tell you more about it.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.49,0:01:24.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.72,0:01:25.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,Hello everyone.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.90,0:01:26.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Welcome.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.57,0:01:35.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm Tanya Das, a director with the energy program at BPC and we are thrilled today to launch a new project on AI and energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:35.44,0:01:39.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I want to take a step back to reflect on how we got here.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.44,0:01:47.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Back in 2019, I was working with colleagues in Congress to develop a bill called the National AI Initiative Act.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.48,0:01:54.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,This was the first bill signed into law that set forth a national AI strategy across our scientific enterprise.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.76,0:02:01.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,The bill contained foundational but modest investments across the federal government on AI R&D.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.32,0:02:07.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,At the time, we didn't have tools like chat GPT, Dolly, or Google AI overviews.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.08,0:02:11.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,AI was very much in our lives already but in more of a quiet way.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.00,0:02:16.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I had no idea how important of a topic AI would become just 5 years later.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.40,0:02:24.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today, we have several billion-dollar bipartisan AI proposals being considered in the House and the Senate.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:24.16,0:02:31.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Members of both sides of the aisle want to double down on using AI to advance science, security, and technology.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.83,0:02:40.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the supercomputers at the Department of Energy are oversubscribed with users eager to try their AI algorithms to accelerate innovation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.00,0:02:47.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And at the center of it all is our energy system that's powering AI and our growing economy.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.36,0:02:51.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,We are seeing electricity load growth for the first time in a decade and
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.52,0:02:56.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,that's occurring at the same time that we're taking serious steps to decarbonize our economy.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.84,0:03:05.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we know AI can be a critical tool to modernize our electric grid, develop new energy technologies, and improve climate resilience.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.80,0:03:10.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And this is why we're launching a new project on the intersection of AI and energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.00,0:03:15.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,We are going to help policy makers identify how to leverage AI to improve our
Dialogue: 0,0:03:15.76,0:03:22.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,energy system while meeting the growing power needs for data centers and other emerging electricity loads.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:22.20,0:03:23.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,As a first step,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.11,0:03:27.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,today we're releasing a report on the pivotal role that the Department of Energy played in
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.01,0:03:33.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,securing US leadership on AI and ideas for how the agency can lead in the future.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:33.96,0:03:40.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is a critical moment for both our AI and energy systems and we need to make sure that we get this right.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.17,0:03:45.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I can't think of two better people to tell us how to do that than our featured speakers today.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.44,0:03:51.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,Please join me in welcoming to the stage my fabulous boss, president and CEO of the Bipartisan Policy Center,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:51.38,0:03:53.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Margaret Spellings and the co-founder,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.70,0:04:01.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,president and CEO of Nvidia,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:01.74,0:04:07.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Jensen Huang.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.20,0:04:12.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,All right, thank you to my fabulous colleague Tanya for that warm welcome and introduction.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.54,0:04:14.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Wow, what a great crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.06,0:04:18.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you all for being here this morning and what a treat it is, Jensen, to be with you.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.92,0:04:20.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm delighted to be I mean, we're thrilled that you're here.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:21.04,0:04:22.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,I was teasing with Jensen, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.32,0:04:30.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,who's known for his famous black leather jacket outfit and I wanted to be in solidarity with him today and he shows up in a suit.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.28,0:04:36.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,The only one he has, by the way, but anyway, um It's the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.48,0:04:38.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm not ashamed of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.72,0:04:40.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a good look.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.23,0:04:41.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, as you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.93,0:04:45.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,we at BPC are proud of the role that we play as a place to bring people
Dialogue: 0,0:04:45.99,0:04:52.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,together to find common ground and to drive towards solutions and there's no thing that needs more thinking,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.38,0:04:57.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,good thinking around that than AI and energy and so we're thrilled to welcome you here today.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.68,0:04:59.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Thank you, Margaret.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:59.09,0:05:03.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, as everybody in the room already knows, but maybe out in TV land they don't,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:03.86,0:05:11.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Jensen is the founder and of Nvidia, a $30 company with about 30,000 employees.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.08,0:05:12.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not quite 30 yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.46,0:05:13.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Not quite 30?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:13.54,0:05:15.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's what I'm I'm sorry, three.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.92,0:05:17.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, what's a zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.21,0:05:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, a zero is a lot in computers but anyway, we'll get into that.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.68,0:05:25.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Anyway, Jensen We're we're working on Yeah, welcome, welcome.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.36,0:05:28.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sorry, I just I'm so impressed with you.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.52,0:05:31.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Anyway, why don't you tell us about how you got started?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.12,0:05:38.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Give us your kind of so-called origin story and how it is that you came to be sitting with me here today.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.32,0:05:40.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,My gosh.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:40.80,0:05:42.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,From where from zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:05:42.72,0:05:48.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, from you know, what the tell us the story of Nvidia, Nvidia and you and Uh we were in Silicon Valley,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:48.12,0:05:54.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,engineers in Silicon Valley designing computers and we observed that there was a better way of designing computers.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.52,0:06:00.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh if you if you um the current computer that we use, it's called general purpose computers.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.96,0:06:06.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,It was invented the year after my birth, 1964 by IBM.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.23,0:06:08.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's called the IBM System 360.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.28,0:06:08.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.75,0:06:14.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it described a central processing unit, CPU, uh multitasking,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.52,0:06:23.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,separation of operating system separation of hardware and software by this layer called operating system, IO subsystems, things like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:23.40,0:06:27.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,And those basic technologies are used today.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.21,0:06:31.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a lot better, a lot faster, but the basic architecture of it is the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:31.32,0:06:33.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a general purpose computer that can do anything.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:33.95,0:06:36.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, we observed 30 years ago, that was 1993,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:36.70,0:06:44.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,we observed that that there are problems that are quite specialized where a general purpose approach is not necessarily the best.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:44.88,0:06:49.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, and and it's no different than if you have generalists do something.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:50.00,0:06:54.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it turns out that software is kind of the same way and there's
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.74,0:07:00.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,a field of software called simulations and physics simulations and data processing and computer graphics.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:01.00,0:07:10.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,These problems, image processing, these problems have algorithms inside that are very computationally intensive.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:10.76,0:07:17.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,And if we could take that and run it on a specialized processor, on a specialized computer,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:17.68,0:07:25.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,we could add a chip to the computer that makes it go 100 times faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:25.69,0:07:30.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,How is it possible to add one chip to another chip and all of a sudden it's 100 times faster?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:30.72,0:07:37.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, the reason for that is of course each chip can focus each computer can focus on what it does best.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:37.84,0:07:42.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we offload and accelerate this thing and we call it accelerated computing.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:42.52,0:07:51.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now, uh the amazing part of it of course is that the domains that we've been able to use it for has been expanding over time.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:51.44,0:07:58.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,And uh it's not it's very routine that we'll accelerate an application 10, 20, 50 times.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:58.46,0:08:05.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um and when you do that, even though you accelerate it by 50 times, since you only added one more chip,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.61,0:08:14.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,you reduce the amount of power by 25 25 times, the amount of energy by 25 times, the amount by 25 times.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:14.37,0:08:20.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so so that this this approach has led to um solving all kinds of interesting problems.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.97,0:08:27.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,The first application we used it for in scientific in in in commercial use was for video games.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.99,0:08:28.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:28.64,0:08:32.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, for a lot of people, Nvidia is still the video game company.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.76,0:08:36.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,We build we build more computers for video games than than any company in the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:36.47,0:08:40.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,When you have when you play PC games, it's probably Nvidia inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:40.60,0:08:42.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,When you play the Nintendo, it's Nvidia inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.80,0:08:45.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so so we're well known for that.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.15,0:08:52.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um but some of our industrial applications, the first one was molecular dynamic simulation for virtual screening, Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.72,0:08:56.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,uh seismic processing for energy discovery.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.25,0:09:06.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then, you know, fluid dynamics, weather simulation, and then one day artificial intelligence found us and and so um accelerated computing,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:06.22,0:09:10.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,that was was an observation about the future computing and it turned out to be right.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:10.57,0:09:18.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so when you extend that into what you call sustainable computing, this is where you get into this savings of energy and being more efficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:18.24,0:09:25.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,that we made is is that a general purpose approach to solving every problem is energy inefficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.02,0:09:26.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:26.52,0:09:28.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're inefficiently doing something.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:28.28,0:09:29.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a little bit like this.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:29.85,0:09:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Suppose suppose you made a factory for building a car.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:35.00,0:09:41.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the assumption that you make is that every single car that you make will be different.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:41.68,0:09:46.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so you have a generalist team of people that, okay, build me a van.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:46.63,0:09:48.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay, now build me a sports car.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:48.55,0:09:50.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay, now build me a truck.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:50.48,0:09:56.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and so because every single one is intended to be different and you want to be generalists,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:56.20,0:10:01.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,you would have a different type of setup than if you all of a sudden
Dialogue: 0,0:10:01.61,0:10:07.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,said to yourself for the next 1 million cars they're going to be exactly the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:07.96,0:10:11.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,The efficiency by which you could build that 1 million cars compared to
Dialogue: 0,0:10:11.93,0:10:18.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,a generalist that would build it one at it's like manufacturing versus crafting.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:18.40,0:10:22.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,The amount of energy you use is going to be substantially lower and so we've
Dialogue: 0,0:10:22.08,0:10:26.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,come to the conclusion we came to the conclusion some 32 years ago that there
Dialogue: 0,0:10:26.56,0:10:34.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,are some parts of the job that needs generalist and you need to do it sequentially to step by step by step you have to reason about it step by step.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:34.88,0:10:42.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,However, there are some parts of the job where you're maybe calculating physics and you're just trying to figure out how molecules move around.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:42.40,0:10:49.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, you're trying to figure out how artificial intelligent neural network is predicting the next token the next word.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:49.24,0:10:58.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,In those type of applications you could have just a mound of processing done at the same time and so this doesn't make sense to you but I'm
Dialogue: 0,0:10:58.48,0:11:07.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,just going to I'm going to say it anyways that the execution of programs called a thread a thread of execution and just follow the follow that thread.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:07.36,0:11:12.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,A CPU can compute one or two threads at one time.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:12.44,0:11:19.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can compute tens of thousands of threads at the same time and so for for work that you can
Dialogue: 0,0:11:19.14,0:11:24.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,break up into a whole bunch of little threads we could get it done a lot faster and you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:24.75,0:11:26.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,as a result much more energy efficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:26.75,0:11:29.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so let's talk more about AI and energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:29.73,0:11:34.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean there's a lot of kind of hysteria going on right now about oh my God this
Dialogue: 0,0:11:34.09,0:11:40.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,this industry this innovation is going to swamp our demand and we're going to have blackouts and you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:40.05,0:11:41.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,this those sorts of things.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:41.80,0:11:43.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Obviously energy abundance is a you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:44.00,0:11:49.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,a key issue for all of us here in the United States especially I'm from Texas so we talk about that a lot
Dialogue: 0,0:11:49.48,0:11:58.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,but give us your thinking on AI and and energy at an Nvidia and more and more broadly how you think about that.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:58.18,0:11:59.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, AI does take energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.63,0:11:59.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.98,0:12:05.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so the first thing to realize that before you can use AI you have to teach an AI.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:06.04,0:12:14.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,This is no different than a when you before you can use an actual intelligence you have to teach the actual intelligence.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:14.81,0:12:20.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so the teaching process consumes a lot of energy and and the reason why it consumes a lot
Dialogue: 0,0:12:20.55,0:12:28.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,of energy is that the artificial intelligence network through trial and error is trying to figure out how to
Dialogue: 0,0:12:28.12,0:12:36.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,predict something and it's recognizing patterns and relationship among tons and tons of information and from all of that
Dialogue: 0,0:12:36.19,0:12:43.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,data it finds out what's the relationship and the pattern such that it can learn a knowledge out of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:43.66,0:12:45.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,We call it representations.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:45.76,0:12:50.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so you're trying to discover knowledge out of data and just
Dialogue: 0,0:12:50.19,0:12:56.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,you're just swimming in it processing it repeatedly looking for that pattern relationships.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:56.28,0:13:03.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,Eventually you can you can you've learned you learned the knowledge that's embedded inside you've learned all these different
Dialogue: 0,0:13:03.90,0:13:11.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,relationships that when you are presented with some future patterns you could understand it you could even predict it.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:11.70,0:13:14.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so so that that's the goal.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:14.18,0:13:19.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,These data centers could consume today maybe 100 megawatts.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:19.80,0:13:26.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And in the future it'll probably be several you know, 10 times 20 times more than that.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:26.52,0:13:30.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,It doesn't have to necessarily be be built in one place but the amount of data
Dialogue: 0,0:13:30.31,0:13:35.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,that we're going to train it with and these AI models are going to use synthetic data
Dialogue: 0,0:13:35.19,0:13:38.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,for example two AI models are going to be talking to each other just like the
Dialogue: 0,0:13:38.79,0:13:44.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,two of us are and just through conversation through Q&A we're going to make each other smarter.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:44.12,0:13:50.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so future future AI models are going to rely on other AI models to learn and
Dialogue: 0,0:13:50.36,0:13:56.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,you could you could use AI models to curate the data so that future AI use AI
Dialogue: 0,0:13:56.57,0:14:00.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,to teach another AI and so there's a lot of a lot of different ways that that
Dialogue: 0,0:14:00.87,0:14:05.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,AI will will will learn in the future but nonetheless it's going to take energy to do so.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:05.72,0:14:12.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,The important thing to realize about AI is that it doesn't it doesn't necessarily care where it learns.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:12.74,0:14:15.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,It doesn't care where it goes to school.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:15.28,0:14:19.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so there are places in the world where we have excess energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:19.32,0:14:27.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's not necessarily connected to the grid it's hard to transport that energy to population but we can transport the data center too.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:27.25,0:14:32.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can build a data center near where there's excess energy and use the energy there.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:32.28,0:14:40.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other the other the big idea about about AI however is the goal of AI is to do things more productively.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:41.00,0:14:45.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,With a lot less energy and there are a whole bunch of examples of this where
Dialogue: 0,0:14:45.88,0:14:51.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,we're using AI one of one of my favorite examples is is using AI to predict weather.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:51.20,0:14:57.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,By training an AI model the physics of weather prediction.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:57.40,0:15:01.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,In the case of weather prediction is mostly atmospheric physics and long
Dialogue: 0,0:15:01.28,0:15:07.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,range weather prediction requires you to understand ocean physics land physics cloud
Dialogue: 0,0:15:07.48,0:15:15.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,physics radiation reflection absorption conduction convection right thermodynamics right fluid dynamics all
Dialogue: 0,0:15:16.00,0:15:22.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,of those forms of physics comes together to to affect the future climate.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:22.05,0:15:29.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, we can teach an AI how to do that through previous observed data and we're starting to make great progress there.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:29.60,0:15:41.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,That AI model can predict weather and predict climate thousands of times tens of thousands of times more energy efficiently.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:41.24,0:15:51.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so the the thing to remember you teach the AI model a few times but you use it continuously everywhere around the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:51.52,0:15:59.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so the benefits of using AI to predict weather instead of using supercomputers to predict weather as we do now can save a ton of energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:00.00,0:16:00.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:00.31,0:16:03.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that's one simple example but the the examples just go on and on.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:03.76,0:16:04.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Right.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:04.14,0:16:08.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's part of the solution as we think about climate not so much part of the problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:08.76,0:16:10.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean It really it really is.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:10.22,0:16:15.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean if you there are there are other examples Margaret is for example that's that's
Dialogue: 0,0:16:15.91,0:16:25.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,one using artificial intelligence to solve problems will consume less energy than using calculation to solve problems.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:25.08,0:16:26.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Right.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:26.17,0:16:28.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay and so that's number one.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:28.96,0:16:32.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Number two we use artificial intelligence now in the smart grid.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:32.55,0:16:33.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know,
Dialogue: 0,0:16:33.19,0:16:39.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,our power grid is not smart but we can make it smart and so we work with a company called Yuli
Dialogue: 0,0:16:39.15,0:16:48.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,data and PG&E to do a to create a an AI computer and we put it integrated into the smart grid.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:48.88,0:16:58.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,The goal is with AI with intelligence in a grid you could figure out how to for example integrate sustainable energy into it.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:58.18,0:17:06.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,That sustainable energy could be from solar it could be the battery sitting in your car during power surges at outages it could
Dialogue: 0,0:17:06.39,0:17:14.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,recognize that there's a weakness in the grid and figure out where the various distributed energy sources are to keep the grid going.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:14.76,0:17:25.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,Maybe you could predict very subtle signals of when the grid is about to fail and send people out to for predictive maintenance.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:25.22,0:17:25.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:25.51,0:17:33.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,You could also do a better job predicting the surges the power surges that's about to happen so you could redirect energy accordingly.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:33.76,0:17:42.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So smart grids smart grids I think is going to make sure help us not over provision in the grid.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:42.24,0:17:47.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, today as we know the grid has the ability to handle a lot more power delivery.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:47.26,0:17:53.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's just that the promise that we made to society is that you're going to get power all the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:53.88,0:17:55.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well,
Dialogue: 0,0:17:55.12,0:18:06.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,unfortunately the power use in population surges extraordinarily could be a factor of two during just a few days of the year.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:06.80,0:18:09.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,The rest of the time it's kind of a lot lower.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:09.93,0:18:18.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,But you have to provision for those days because people need the power need the energy need the heat or cooling to stay alive.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:18.07,0:18:25.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so so we could we could redirect if the grid was smart we could redirect
Dialogue: 0,0:18:25.57,0:18:30.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,the energy in a smarter way and so there's a there's lots and lots of examples.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:30.56,0:18:32.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're a really good teacher.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:32.48,0:18:36.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,So here we are in Washington and obviously we're all about federal policy.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:36.10,0:18:43.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,The federal government has been an important partner on innovating on some of the things that you're talking about.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:43.31,0:18:50.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do you see that that history your work with with the US Department of Energy and then where we ought to go next?
Dialogue: 0,0:18:50.32,0:18:55.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean just give your sense of you know, if you were if you're running the Department of Energy and had a checkbook you know,
